export default JSON.parse("{\"1.7.1\":{\"unit\":\"1.7\",\"courseId\":\"1.7.1\",\"bridge\":\"Unit 1.6 gave you a plan, a journal with an emotion field (1.6.4) and process KPIs (1.6.7). Rules only help if they hold when feelings run high. Here you learn to name and rate those feelings, so the plan still decides.\",\"hook\":{\"prompt\":\"Your open long is up 150.00 vUSD and rising fast. What do you feel most?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"add\",\"label\":\"Excitement: time to add more\"},{\"id\":\"fear\",\"label\":\"Fear of giving it back\"},{\"id\":\"calm\",\"label\":\"Calm: the plan decides\"},{\"id\":\"hope\",\"label\":\"Hope it goes much higher\"}]},\"compare\":\"You chose “{yours}”. Everyone feels these. What matters is what the plan does next: here, the stop and the target you set at entry.\",\"concept\":\"emotions-in-trading\"},\"learn\":[{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"Signals, not flaws\",\"text\":\"Fear, greed, hope and regret show up in every trader, beginner or professional. They are normal signals, not personal flaws. The goal isn't to stop feeling them; nobody manages that. It's to notice them early and to have rules that hold when they arrive.\"},{\"kind\":\"match\",\"title\":\"Name the feeling\",\"prompt\":\"Four moments every trader meets. Match each one to the feeling it usually brings.\",\"pairs\":[{\"left\":\"Price nears your stop\",\"right\":\"Fear\"},{\"left\":\"Up a lot, urge to add without a setup\",\"right\":\"Greed\",\"wrong\":\"Fear pulls you out of a trade. Wanting more, with no setup to justify it, is greed.\"},{\"left\":\"Holding a loser: 'it'll come back'\",\"right\":\"Hope\",\"wrong\":\"Hope keeps a loser open on a wish. Fear would push you to close it.\"},{\"left\":\"You skipped a setup and it ran without you\",\"right\":\"Regret\",\"wrong\":\"Regret looks back at a choice you made, and often pushes you to make up for it.\"}],\"concept\":\"emotions-in-trading\"},{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"What one study found\",\"text\":\"Lo, Repin and Steenbarger (2005) followed 80 day traders for five weeks. Those with more intense emotional reactions, to gains and to losses alike, had significantly worse results. No single 'trader personality' stood out. It's one small study, but the practical point holds: strong reactions are worth noticing, whatever set them off.\"},{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"Fit to trade\",\"text\":\"Too little arousal and too much both hurt decisions. Tiredness, poor sleep, illness and stress are reasons to trade smaller or not at all. Studies of traders' stress hormones are small (one followed 17 traders for eight days), so treat this as a sensible rule, not a proven effect. It goes in your plan as a [[fit-to-trade]] line.\"},{\"kind\":\"order\",\"title\":\"When a strong urge shows up\",\"prompt\":\"A strong urge to act is a cue to slow down, not a reason to trade. Put the steps in order.\",\"items\":[\"Notice it\",\"Name it\",\"Rate it, 1 to 5\",\"Check the plan\",\"Decide\"],\"explain\":\"That's the sequence. Naming and rating take seconds, and they put a gap between the feeling and the order ticket.\",\"concept\":\"emotions-in-trading\"},{\"kind\":\"predict\",\"title\":\"Practise a check-in\",\"prompt\":\"Picture your next entry, ten minutes after a stop-out. How strong is the feeling, from 1 (calm) to 5 (very strong)? There's no right answer: this is a check-in.\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"slider\",\"min\":1,\"max\":5,\"step\":1,\"unit\":\"of 5\",\"start\":3,\"feedback\":[{\"from\":1,\"to\":2,\"text\":\"Low. Run the checklist as usual and write the number in your journal.\"},{\"from\":3,\"to\":3,\"text\":\"Moderate. Note it, and let the checklist make the decision.\"},{\"from\":4,\"to\":5,\"text\":\"Strong. That's the cue to pause before any decision. The last lesson of this unit gives the pause a length and a routine.\"}]},\"reveal\":\"Check in twice per trade: before entry and after exit, with a number from 1 to 5 and a word. Over 30 trades, your [[journal]] shows whether high numbers go with broken rules. Compare the ratings with rule-following, not with profit.\",\"concept\":\"emotions-in-trading\"},{\"kind\":\"fields\",\"title\":\"Your fit-to-trade line\",\"prompt\":\"Write the [[fit-to-trade]] line of your plan: when you count as not fit, and what your risk is on those days. Not fit means smaller or nothing.\",\"fields\":[{\"id\":\"sleep\",\"label\":\"Not fit if I slept less than __ hours\",\"kind\":\"stepper\",\"min\":4,\"max\":8,\"step\":1,\"default\":6},{\"id\":\"rating\",\"label\":\"Not fit if my check-in is __ or higher\",\"kind\":\"stepper\",\"min\":3,\"max\":5,\"step\":1,\"default\":4},{\"id\":\"risk\",\"label\":\"Risk per trade on a day I'm not fit\",\"kind\":\"choice\",\"options\":[0,0.5,1],\"unit\":\"%\",\"default\":1}],\"valid\":{\"field\":\"risk\",\"lte\":0.5},\"correct\":\"Saved. On a day that fails this line, you trade smaller or not at all, decided now, while you're calm.\",\"feedback\":[{\"when\":{\"field\":\"risk\",\"gt\":0.5},\"text\":\"1% is your usual risk, so nothing changes on a bad day. Not fit to trade means smaller or nothing: choose 0.5% or 0%.\"}],\"concept\":\"emotions-in-trading\"},{\"kind\":\"callout\",\"tone\":\"key\",\"text\":\"An emotion is information about you, not about the market.\"}],\"task\":{\"widget\":\"bias-spotter\",\"data\":{\"cards\":[{\"id\":\"t1-before\",\"text\":\"Trade 1, before entry. The pullback setup is complete, the stop is set at 1% risk and you slept well. 'The plan covers this.'\",\"answer\":\"calm\",\"correct\":\"Calm. A check-in here might read 1 or 2 out of 5. Note it anyway: calm days are the baseline you compare against.\",\"rationales\":{\"fear\":\"Nothing here signals danger: the setup is complete and the risk is set. This is what calm looks like.\",\"greed\":\"No urge for more size or more trades. The plan sets the risk and you're following it.\",\"hope\":\"Hope wishes a trade into working. Here the decision rests on a complete setup, not a wish.\",\"regret\":\"Nothing is being looked back on. This is a fresh decision, made by the plan.\"}},{\"id\":\"t1-after\",\"text\":\"Trade 1, after the exit. Stopped out at −1R. Ten minutes later price turns and runs to where your target was. 'If only I'd held on.'\",\"answer\":\"regret\",\"correct\":\"Regret. It looks back and tempts you to widen the next stop or chase the move. The stop was right when you set it.\",\"rationales\":{\"fear\":\"Fear looks ahead at a possible loss. This feeling looks back at a choice already made.\",\"greed\":\"It may turn into chasing, but the feeling itself is about the closed trade, not about wanting more.\",\"hope\":\"Hope keeps a trade open. This one is closed; the feeling is about what might have been.\",\"calm\":\"'If only' is a strong reaction to a result. A calm check-in wouldn't replay the exit.\"}},{\"id\":\"t2-before\",\"text\":\"Trade 2, before entry. You're up 150.00 vUSD on the day. There's no setup, but you think: 'I'm on a roll. Double the size and catch the rest.'\",\"answer\":\"greed\",\"correct\":\"Greed: wanting more, with no setup to justify it. A strong reading here is a reason to stop, not to double.\",\"rationales\":{\"fear\":\"Nothing here is a threat. The pull is towards more size and more profit.\",\"hope\":\"Hope protects a losing trade. Here you're winning and want more: that's greed.\",\"regret\":\"Nothing was missed or lost. The urge is to add, driven by feeling sharp.\",\"calm\":\"Doubling the size without a setup isn't calm. It's a strong feeling taking over the plan.\"}},{\"id\":\"t2-exit\",\"text\":\"Trade 2, at the exit. Up 0.4R, price ticks down once and you close straight away, well before your target: 'I can't give this back.'\",\"answer\":\"fear\",\"correct\":\"Fear. The exit came from the feeling, not from the plan. Rating it now helps you spot the pattern later in your journal.\",\"rationales\":{\"greed\":\"Greed would hold on for more. Here you grab a small gain so as not to lose it.\",\"hope\":\"Hope keeps a trade open. This is the opposite: closing early to escape the risk.\",\"regret\":\"Regret may come later. In the moment, the feeling is fear of giving the gain back.\",\"calm\":\"A calm exit follows the plan's target or stop. Closing on one tick is a reaction.\"}},{\"id\":\"t3-before\",\"text\":\"Trade 3, before entry. Two losses so far today. A valid setup appears, but your hand hovers over the ticket: 'What if this one loses too?'\",\"answer\":\"fear\",\"correct\":\"Fear. It can make you skip valid setups after losses. If the rating is high, the pause decides, not the hesitation.\",\"rationales\":{\"greed\":\"Nothing here is about wanting more. The worry is about losing again.\",\"hope\":\"Hope wishes for a good result. Here you're braced for a bad one.\",\"regret\":\"The two losses are behind you, but the feeling points ahead, at the next one.\",\"calm\":\"A hand hovering over the ticket and 'what if' aren't calm. That's worry about the next loss.\"}},{\"id\":\"t3-hold\",\"text\":\"Trade 3, holding. The trade is at −0.8R, close to the stop. You drag the stop lower: 'It always comes back.'\",\"answer\":\"hope\",\"correct\":\"Hope. It keeps a loser open on a wish. The rule from 'FOMO, revenge and loss aversion': never widen a stop.\",\"rationales\":{\"fear\":\"Price near the stop often brings fear. But dragging the stop on 'it always comes back' is a wish: hope.\",\"greed\":\"Nothing here is about wanting more. It's about not accepting a loss.\",\"regret\":\"You're not looking back at a past choice. You're betting on a wish about the future.\",\"calm\":\"Moving the stop to avoid a loss is a reaction, not a calm decision.\"}}],\"options\":[{\"id\":\"fear\",\"label\":\"Fear\"},{\"id\":\"greed\",\"label\":\"Greed\"},{\"id\":\"hope\",\"label\":\"Hope\"},{\"id\":\"regret\",\"label\":\"Regret\"},{\"id\":\"calm\",\"label\":\"Calm\"}]},\"goal\":\"Three simulated trades, six moments. Read each one and name the feeling behind it, as you would in a check-in.\",\"success\":{\"field\":\"solved\",\"eq\":6},\"correct\":\"All six named. In the Arena, a check-in adds a number from 1 to 5. Over time, compare those numbers with rule-following, not with profit.\",\"hints\":[\"Look at the inner voice in quotes: it usually gives the feeling away.\",\"Looking back at a past choice is regret. Wanting more without a setup is greed.\",\"Closing early or freezing to escape a loss is fear. Keeping a loser open on a wish is hope.\"],\"showMe\":{\"solved\":6},\"concept\":\"emotions-in-trading\"},\"check\":[\"q-1.7.1-1\",\"q-1.7.1-2\",\"q-1.7.1-3\"],\"recap\":{\"takeaways\":[\"Fear, greed, hope and regret are normal. Name the feeling; the plan still decides.\",\"Check in before entry and after exit: a number from 1 to 5 and a word.\",\"Strong reactions to gains and losses go with worse results, so notice them and slow down.\"],\"next\":\"Next, 1.7.2: the three pulls that do the most damage, FOMO, revenge and loss aversion, and a written rule for each.\"}},\"1.7.3\":{\"unit\":\"1.7\",\"courseId\":\"1.7.3\",\"bridge\":\"In 1.7.2 you met revenge sizing: trading bigger to win a loss back. In 0.2.3 you saw that long streaks are normal. Here the two meet: a streak doesn't make a win due or make you hot, so your risk per trade stays the same.\",\"hook\":{\"prompt\":\"Six losses in a row on a setup that wins 50% of the time. What's the chance the next one wins?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"more\",\"label\":\"More than 50%: it's due\"},{\"id\":\"fifty\",\"label\":\"50%\"},{\"id\":\"less\",\"label\":\"Less than 50%\"}],\"answer\":\"fifty\"},\"compare\":\"You chose “{yours}”. If results are independent, it's 50%: the setup doesn't remember the last six. A setup can stop working, but the streak alone doesn't show it.\",\"concept\":\"gamblers-fallacy\"},\"learn\":[{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"Two mirror images\",\"text\":\"The [[gamblers-fallacy|gambler's fallacy]] expects a streak to reverse: 'six losses, a win is due.' Its mirror image, the [[hot-hand|hot-hand fallacy]], expects it to continue: 'five wins, I'm on fire.' Both feel like insight. With independent outcomes both are wrong, and both usually end in the same move: a bigger size.\"},{\"kind\":\"figure\",\"title\":\"Does the coin remember?\",\"text\":\"A fair coin, flipped 1,000,000 times by a simulator. Each time five tails came up in a row, we looked at the next flip.\",\"figure\":{\"kind\":\"bars\",\"unit\":\"%\",\"bars\":[{\"label\":\"Heads, all flips\",\"value\":50,\"role\":\"neutral\"},{\"label\":\"Heads after 5 tails\",\"value\":49.7,\"role\":\"up\"},{\"label\":\"Tails after 5 tails\",\"value\":50.3,\"role\":\"down\"}],\"steps\":[\"Across all 1,000,000 flips, heads came up 50.0% of the time.\",\"Five tails in a row came up 31,405 times. Next flip: heads 49.7%.\",\"Tails 50.3%. The streak changed nothing.\"]},\"concept\":\"gamblers-fallacy\"},{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"Real people, real bets\",\"text\":\"The pull is strong outside the lab too. In casino roulette data, after six spins of the same colour, about 85% of bettors bet on the other colour, as if it were due. That figure comes from a secondary summary, but the pattern is a familiar one: a streak feels like information.\"},{\"kind\":\"predict\",\"title\":\"On a roll\",\"prompt\":\"You've won five trades in a row at 1% risk. Do you double your size for the next trade?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"yes\",\"label\":\"Yes, I'm on fire\",\"feedback\":\"That's the hot hand. Five wins don't change the next trade's odds, and doubling turns the next normal loss into −2%.\"},{\"id\":\"no\",\"label\":\"No, risk stays at 1%\",\"feedback\":\"Right. The size comes from the plan, not from the last five results.\"}],\"answer\":\"no\"},\"reveal\":\"Five wins can be skill, luck or both, and five trades can't tell which. The next trade is a new draw. Size it from the plan, never from the streak.\",\"concept\":\"gamblers-fallacy\"},{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"Markets aren't coins\",\"text\":\"Market results aren't perfect coin flips: they can cluster when conditions change, and whether real streaks exist is an empirical question (Miller and Sanjurjo). So don't answer a streak with a feeling. Answer it with a plan rule: a full review after a set number of losses, judged on your [[journal]], not on the last few results.\"},{\"kind\":\"match\",\"title\":\"Thought or trap?\",\"prompt\":\"Streaks start loud thoughts. Match each thought to the trap behind it.\",\"pairs\":[{\"left\":\"'I'm due a win.'\",\"right\":\"Gambler's fallacy\"},{\"left\":\"'I'm on fire, size up.'\",\"right\":\"Hot hand\",\"wrong\":\"The gambler's fallacy expects a reversal. 'Size up' expects the streak to continue: the hot hand.\"},{\"left\":\"'Three losses: this setup is dead.'\",\"right\":\"Small sample\",\"wrong\":\"Three trades can't tell a broken setup from a normal run of losses. The problem is the sample size.\"},{\"left\":\"'Five wins: I've cracked it.'\",\"right\":\"Small sample and overconfidence\",\"wrong\":\"Five trades can't prove an edge either, and feeling you've 'cracked it' adds overconfidence.\"}],\"concept\":\"gamblers-fallacy\"},{\"kind\":\"worked\",\"title\":\"What doubling after a loss costs\",\"lines\":[\"A fair coin, 100.00 vUSD per flip, account 10,000.00. Six losses in a row are more likely than not somewhere in 100 flips.\",\"Fixed size: 6 × 100.00 = 600.00 lost. Account: 9,400.00.\",\"Doubling after each loss: 100 + 200 + 400 + 800 + 1,600 + 3,200 = ?\"],\"ask\":{\"answer\":6300,\"tolerance\":0,\"unit\":\"vUSD\",\"feedback\":[{\"value\":3200,\"text\":\"That's the sixth bet alone. Add all six: 100 + 200 + 400 + 800 + 1,600 + 3,200.\"},{\"value\":600,\"text\":\"That's the fixed size. With doubling, each bet is twice the one before.\"}],\"correct\":\"Right: 6,300.00, against 600.00 at a fixed size. The account is down to 3,700.00, and the seventh bet would need 6,400.00.\"},\"after\":\"Doubling after losses is a bet that a win is due. It has a name, martingale, and the next unit shows where it leads. A fixed size keeps a streak survivable.\",\"concept\":\"gamblers-fallacy\"},{\"kind\":\"callout\",\"tone\":\"key\",\"text\":\"Risk per trade stays the same after wins and after losses.\"}],\"task\":{\"widget\":\"expectancy\",\"goal\":\"Your setup wins 50% of trades at +1.5R and loses 1R: +0.25R per trade. Find the longest losing run that is still more likely than not over 100 trades.\",\"start\":{\"winRate\":50,\"avgWin\":1.5,\"avgLoss\":1,\"runLength\":4},\"locked\":[\"winRate\",\"avgWin\",\"avgLoss\"],\"success\":{\"all\":[{\"field\":\"runLength\",\"eq\":6},{\"field\":\"winRate\",\"eq\":50}]},\"feedback\":[{\"when\":{\"field\":\"runLength\",\"lt\":6},\"text\":\"A run of {runLength} is {runProb:1}% likely over 100 trades: more likely than not. Try a longer run.\"},{\"when\":{\"field\":\"runLength\",\"gt\":6},\"text\":\"A run of {runLength} is {runProb:1}% likely: less likely than not. Step back until it's above 50%.\"}],\"correct\":\"Right. Six losses in a row is 54.6% likely over 100 trades of this setup, even at +0.25R per trade. Six losses alone don't show it broke, and the next trade is still 50%.\",\"hints\":[\"'More likely than not' means a chance above 50%.\",\"Step the run length up one at a time and watch the chance fall.\",\"A run of 7 is 31.8% likely. Try one shorter.\"],\"showMe\":{\"runLength\":6},\"concept\":\"gamblers-fallacy\"},\"check\":[\"q-1.7.3-1\",\"q-1.7.3-2\",\"q-1.7.3-3\"],\"recap\":{\"takeaways\":[\"With independent results, a losing run doesn't make a win due. The next trade is a new draw.\",\"Sizing up after wins or after losses lets the streak decide, not the plan.\",\"Risk per trade stays the same through streaks. A plan rule decides when to review.\"],\"next\":\"Next, 1.7.4: outcome bias in your own trades, where one lucky win can teach you a broken rule.\"}},\"1.7.4\":{\"unit\":\"1.7\",\"courseId\":\"1.7.4\",\"bridge\":\"1.7.3 kept your risk fixed through streaks; this lesson keeps your rules fixed through lucky and unlucky results. In 0.2.1 you judged decisions by process, and in 1.6.5 you reviewed your own. Here you grade the process first, then the result.\",\"hook\":{\"prompt\":\"You moved your stop away and the trade came back to +2R. Next time price nears your stop, what are you more likely to do?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"move\",\"label\":\"Move it again\"},{\"id\":\"leave\",\"label\":\"Leave it\"},{\"id\":\"unsure\",\"label\":\"Not sure\"}]},\"compare\":\"You chose “{yours}”. A profit just rewarded a broken rule. That's how bad habits get stronger, and why the grade comes before the result.\",\"concept\":\"decision-quality\"},\"learn\":[{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"Knowing isn't enough\",\"text\":\"Outcome bias is judging a decision by how it turned out. Knowing about it doesn't switch it off: a 2023 pre-registered replication with 692 people found it again, even among those who said outcomes shouldn't count. The fix isn't more willpower. It's an order: grade the process first, look at the result second.\"},{\"kind\":\"figure\",\"title\":\"Two loops\",\"text\":\"Results train habits, for better or worse. Walk this case through: you widened your stop, and the trade came back to +2R.\",\"figure\":{\"kind\":\"flow\",\"start\":\"n1\",\"interactive\":true,\"nodes\":[{\"id\":\"n1\",\"text\":\"Rule followed?\",\"kind\":\"question\",\"yes\":\"n2\",\"no\":\"n3\",\"correct\":\"no\",\"explain\":\"Widening the stop broke the plan, whatever happened next.\"},{\"id\":\"n2\",\"text\":\"Trade lost?\",\"kind\":\"question\",\"yes\":\"drop\",\"no\":\"repeat\"},{\"id\":\"n3\",\"text\":\"Trade won?\",\"kind\":\"question\",\"yes\":\"reward\",\"no\":\"name\",\"correct\":\"yes\",\"explain\":\"+2R is a win. That's the box where a broken habit gets paid.\"},{\"id\":\"drop\",\"text\":\"Danger: you drop a sound rule\",\"kind\":\"end\"},{\"id\":\"repeat\",\"text\":\"Note what made it sound\",\"kind\":\"end\"},{\"id\":\"reward\",\"text\":\"Danger: the broken habit gets stronger\",\"kind\":\"end\"},{\"id\":\"name\",\"text\":\"Name the rule you broke\",\"kind\":\"end\"}],\"steps\":[\"Rule broken, trade won: the profit rewards the habit. The most dangerous box.\",\"Rule followed, trade lost: the loss tempts you to drop a sound rule.\",\"Both loops start the same way: letting the result grade the decision.\"]},\"concept\":\"decision-quality\"},{\"kind\":\"predict\",\"title\":\"Grade before the reveal\",\"prompt\":\"Grade this trade before you see its result. Every playbook condition met, risk 1% with costs, stop set at entry and never moved, closed by the plan's time limit.\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"sound\",\"label\":\"Sound process\",\"feedback\":\"Right. Every rule was followed, so the process grade is good, whatever the number says.\"},{\"id\":\"broken\",\"label\":\"A broken rule\",\"feedback\":\"Check each rule: conditions met, risk within 1%, stop untouched, exit by the plan. Nothing was broken.\"}],\"answer\":\"sound\"},\"reveal\":\"The result: −0.4R. A sound decision with a small loss. Because you graded first, the number can't rewrite the grade. MARKET//LAB's [[decision-quality|Decision Quality]] score works the same way.\",\"concept\":\"decision-quality\"},{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"Skill or luck?\",\"text\":\"A close cousin is self-attribution: wins feel like skill, losses like bad luck. Lucky wins then teach the wrong lesson and unlucky losses teach none. Unit 2.12 covers it in full. For now, one habit counters both: rate each trade's process before you see its profit or loss.\"},{\"kind\":\"predict\",\"title\":\"What's the evidence?\",\"prompt\":\"Three trades in a row followed every rule and lost. A friend says: 'Your stop is too tight, widen it.' What's the evidence for the change?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"three\",\"label\":\"Three losses\",\"feedback\":\"Three trades are a tiny sample: a sound rule often loses three in a row. The losses alone can't show the stop is wrong.\"},{\"id\":\"tiny\",\"label\":\"Not enough: three trades are a tiny sample\",\"feedback\":\"Right.\"}],\"answer\":\"tiny\"},\"reveal\":\"Three rule-following losses are what [[variance]] looks like: results scattering by chance around the rule's average. A change to the plan goes through its own rule for changes: enough trades, reviewed in the journal, not a reaction to the last three.\",\"concept\":\"decision-quality\"},{\"kind\":\"callout\",\"tone\":\"key\",\"text\":\"Grade first. Look at the P&L second.\"}],\"task\":{\"widget\":\"bias-spotter\",\"data\":{\"cards\":[{\"id\":\"g1\",\"text\":\"Pullback long. All three conditions met, risk 1% with costs, stop set at entry and left alone, exit by the plan.\",\"answer\":\"followed\",\"correct\":\"Followed the plan. The result: −1R. A sound trade that lost. Nothing to fix.\",\"rationales\":{\"broke\":\"Check the rules one by one: conditions met, risk within 1%, stop untouched, exit by the plan. Nothing was broken.\"}},{\"id\":\"g2\",\"text\":\"Two of the three conditions met, 'but it looked strong'. Risk 1%, stop left alone, exit by the plan.\",\"answer\":\"broke\",\"correct\":\"Broke a rule: the playbook needs every condition. The result: +1.8R. A lucky win on a broken rule, the most dangerous box.\",\"rationales\":{\"followed\":\"Risk and stop were fine, but the playbook needs all three conditions. Entering on two broke the entry rule.\"}},{\"id\":\"g3\",\"text\":\"All conditions met, risk 1%. When price neared the stop, you moved it 1.00 further away. Exit by the plan.\",\"answer\":\"broke\",\"correct\":\"Broke a rule: the stop was widened. The result: +2.0R. The profit rewards the habit; the grade doesn't.\",\"rationales\":{\"followed\":\"The entry was fine, but moving the stop further away broke the plan and raised the risk above 1%.\"}},{\"id\":\"g4\",\"text\":\"All conditions met, risk 0.9% with costs, stop set at entry and never moved, exit by the plan.\",\"answer\":\"followed\",\"correct\":\"Followed the plan. The result: +2.1R. A sound trade that won: repeat what made it sound, not the profit.\",\"rationales\":{\"broke\":\"Every rule was kept: three conditions, risk under 1%, stop untouched, exit by the plan.\"}},{\"id\":\"g5\",\"text\":\"Third trade of the day, after two losses. The setup is complete, but you doubled your size 'to get back to even'.\",\"answer\":\"broke\",\"correct\":\"Broke a rule: risk never rises after a loss. The result: −2R. Bad decision, bad outcome: name the rule and keep it.\",\"rationales\":{\"followed\":\"The setup was complete, but doubling the size after losses broke the risk rule: 2% instead of 1%.\"}}],\"options\":[{\"id\":\"followed\",\"label\":\"Followed the plan\"},{\"id\":\"broke\",\"label\":\"Broke a rule\"}]},\"goal\":\"Five trades from a journal, with the result hidden. Grade each one's process first; the profit or loss appears only after your grade.\",\"success\":{\"field\":\"solved\",\"eq\":5},\"correct\":\"Five grades before five results. Two wins came from broken rules and one loss from a sound trade: grading by P&L would have got three of five wrong.\",\"hints\":[\"Ignore any guess about the result. Check the rules: conditions, risk, stop, exit.\",\"One broken rule makes the grade 'Broke a rule', even if the rest was fine.\",\"Watch for entries on fewer than three conditions, moved stops and bigger sizes after losses.\"],\"showMe\":{\"solved\":5},\"concept\":\"decision-quality\"},\"check\":[\"q-1.7.4-1\",\"q-1.7.4-2\",\"q-1.7.4-3\"],\"recap\":{\"takeaways\":[\"Grade the process before you look at the result: setup, risk, stop, exit.\",\"A lucky win on a broken rule is the most dangerous box: it trains the habit.\",\"Unlucky losses on sound rules are variance. Change rules only through the plan's change rule.\"],\"next\":\"Next, 1.7.5: boredom and overtrading, the trades taken because nothing was happening, and what they cost in R.\"}},\"1.7.5\":{\"unit\":\"1.7\",\"courseId\":\"1.7.5\",\"bridge\":\"1.7.4 graded trades you took. This lesson looks at trades you shouldn't take at all: boredom trades outside the plan. In 1.4.6 you measured costs in R; here you add them up across extra trades and see why waiting counts as a decision.\",\"hook\":{\"prompt\":\"Three quiet hours, no setup. A small move appears that isn't in your plan. What do you do?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"small\",\"label\":\"Take it, small\"},{\"id\":\"wait\",\"label\":\"Wait\"},{\"id\":\"relax\",\"label\":\"Relax my rules for today\"}]},\"compare\":\"You chose “{yours}”. The urge to do something after hours of nothing is normal. The plan answers it: a move that isn't a setup isn't a trade, at any size.\",\"concept\":\"action-bias\"},\"learn\":[{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"Doing something\",\"text\":\"[[action-bias|Action bias]] is the preference for doing something over waiting, even when waiting is better. Boredom feeds it: after hours with no setup, a small move starts to look like one. [[overtrading|Overtrading]] is trading more often than your setups allow, often to relieve boredom or to win back a loss.\"},{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"What frequency costs\",\"text\":\"Barber and Odean studied 66,465 US households. The most active fifth earned 11.4% a year, against 17.9% for the market; trading costs and frequency, not stock picking, explained the gap. They were stock investors, not day traders, but the arithmetic carries over: every extra trade pays costs, edge or not.\"},{\"kind\":\"worked\",\"title\":\"Off-plan trades in R\",\"lines\":[\"An off-plan trade has no edge: before costs, it wins and loses about equally.\",\"Costs are certain: a round trip on 50 LABX units at 100.00 costs 10.01 vUSD, about 0.10R when 1R is 100.00.\",\"Four extra trades a day: 4 × 0.10R = 0.40R per day.\",\"Over 20 trading days: 20 × 0.40R = ?\"],\"ask\":{\"answer\":8,\"tolerance\":0,\"unit\":\"R\",\"feedback\":[{\"value\":0.4,\"text\":\"That's one day. Multiply by the 20 trading days.\"},{\"value\":80,\"text\":\"Check the daily figure: 4 × 0.10R is 0.40R, not 4R.\"}],\"correct\":\"Right: 8R. Eight times your risk per trade, paid for nothing.\"},\"after\":\"At 1% risk per trade, that's roughly 8% of the account, gone before any edge. Quiet sessions make it worse: small moves leave little room above costs.\",\"concept\":\"overtrading\"},{\"kind\":\"match\",\"title\":\"An urge, a rule\",\"prompt\":\"Each urge to trade more has a rule that answers it. Match them.\",\"pairs\":[{\"left\":\"Bored, nothing happening\",\"right\":\"A move that isn't in the plan isn't a trade\"},{\"left\":\"Two wins, feeling sharp\",\"right\":\"Max trades per day\",\"wrong\":\"Feeling sharp after wins invites extra trades. A daily maximum caps them before the feeling decides.\"},{\"left\":\"Down on the day, want it back\",\"right\":\"Pause, then the daily limit\",\"wrong\":\"Wanting a loss back is the revenge pattern. A pause comes first, and the daily limit ends the day.\"},{\"left\":\"Small target, many trades\",\"right\":\"Check the target against costs\",\"wrong\":\"With many small targets, costs take a big share of each one. Check the target clears costs first.\"}],\"concept\":\"overtrading\"},{\"kind\":\"predict\",\"title\":\"Is waiting worth anything?\",\"prompt\":\"In MARKET//LAB, does a well-reasoned 'no trade' earn anything?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"no\",\"label\":\"No: nothing happened\",\"feedback\":\"Something did happen: you made a decision. The simulator scores decisions, and 'no trade, no setup' is one.\"},{\"id\":\"yes\",\"label\":\"Yes: Decision Quality\",\"feedback\":\"Right.\"}],\"answer\":\"yes\"},\"reveal\":\"[[decision-quality|Decision Quality]] scores the decision, not the activity. A logged 'no trade: no setup' is a good decision, and the mission No Trade Is a Trade is built on it. Sitting on your hands is a skill you can practise.\",\"concept\":\"action-bias\"},{\"kind\":\"callout\",\"tone\":\"key\",\"text\":\"In quiet markets, costs take the biggest bite out of small moves. Waiting is a position.\"}],\"task\":{\"widget\":\"bias-spotter\",\"data\":{\"cards\":[{\"id\":\"s1\",\"text\":\"10:05. Two hours of tiny candles. A 0.30 pop above the morning high, with no pullback and nothing from your playbook.\",\"answer\":\"skip\",\"correct\":\"Skip. A pop isn't a setup. Logging 'no trade: no setup' is the decision.\",\"rationales\":{\"take\":\"Not in your playbook. That's a trade for boredom, not for a reason.\"}},{\"id\":\"s2\",\"text\":\"10:40. Higher highs and higher lows. The pullback holds above the last swing low, and the previous high is 2.4R away.\",\"answer\":\"take\",\"correct\":\"Take it. Every condition is met. This is the trade the waiting was for.\",\"rationales\":{\"skip\":\"Every condition is met and the target clears 2R. Skipping valid setups is an error too: the plan only works if you take them.\"}},{\"id\":\"s3\",\"text\":\"11:20. Price drifts sideways in a tight range. A chat message says 'LABX looks ready to go'.\",\"answer\":\"skip\",\"correct\":\"Skip. A range and a tip aren't a setup. Boredom makes other people's ideas look tempting.\",\"rationales\":{\"take\":\"Not in your playbook. That's a trade for boredom, not for a reason.\"}},{\"id\":\"s4\",\"text\":\"12:10. Price dips 0.20 and bounces. You think: 'At least I'd be doing something.'\",\"answer\":\"skip\",\"correct\":\"Skip. 'Doing something' is action bias talking. Waiting is the position.\",\"rationales\":{\"take\":\"Not in your playbook. That's a trade for boredom, not for a reason.\"}},{\"id\":\"s5\",\"text\":\"13:30. A pullback in the uptrend holds above the last swing low, but the previous high is only 1.5R away.\",\"answer\":\"skip\",\"correct\":\"Skip. Two conditions out of three: the target is below 2R. Close isn't a setup.\",\"rationales\":{\"take\":\"Close, but condition 3 fails: 1.5R is below the 2R minimum. Two of three is no trade.\"}},{\"id\":\"s6\",\"text\":\"14:15. A new higher high, then a pullback that holds above the last swing low. The previous high is 2.1R away and the risk fits 1%.\",\"answer\":\"take\",\"correct\":\"Take it. All three conditions, risk within the limit. Two trades in a quiet day is what the plan allowed.\",\"rationales\":{\"skip\":\"Every condition is met and 2.1R clears the minimum. A valid setup is the reason to trade, and this is one.\"}}],\"options\":[{\"id\":\"take\",\"label\":\"Take it\"},{\"id\":\"skip\",\"label\":\"Skip it\"}]},\"goal\":\"A quiet session, six decision points. Your playbook: a pullback in an uptrend, every condition met, target at least 2R. Take the valid setups and skip the rest.\",\"success\":{\"field\":\"solved\",\"eq\":6},\"correct\":\"Two trades, four skips, all by the plan. In the simulator the skips count too: each well-reasoned 'no trade' is a good decision.\",\"hints\":[\"Check each moment against the playbook: uptrend, pullback holding above the last swing low, target at least 2R.\",\"A feeling, a tip or a small pop is not a condition. If any condition fails, skip.\",\"Two moments meet all three conditions: 10:40 and 14:15.\"],\"showMe\":{\"solved\":6},\"concept\":\"overtrading\"},\"check\":[\"q-1.7.5-1\",\"q-1.7.5-2\",\"q-1.7.5-3\"],\"recap\":{\"takeaways\":[\"Boredom trades come from action bias, not from a setup. A move outside the plan isn't a trade.\",\"At about 0.10R in costs each, four extra trades a day cost 8R over 20 days.\",\"A well-reasoned 'no trade' is a scored decision. Waiting is a position.\"],\"next\":\"Next, 1.7.6: turn each urge into an if-then rule with a trigger you can observe, and write it into your plan.\"}},\"1.7.6\":{\"unit\":\"1.7\",\"courseId\":\"1.7.6\",\"bridge\":\"1.7.2 gave you a rule for each trap, and 1.7.5 matched each urge to trade more with a rule. Here you rewrite them as if-then rules, with a trigger you can observe, and add them to your plan v1 from 1.6.2.\",\"hook\":{\"prompt\":\"Which rule is more likely to hold after a painful loss?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"calm\",\"label\":\"Stay calm and don't revenge trade\"},{\"id\":\"ifthen\",\"label\":\"If a trade hits its stop, then I close the ticket and wait 30 minutes before the next decision\"}],\"answer\":\"ifthen\"},\"compare\":\"You chose “{yours}”. The second rule holds better: it names a trigger you can see and an action you can do, so there's nothing left to decide in the moment.\",\"concept\":\"if-then-rules\"},\"learn\":[{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"If X, then Y\",\"text\":\"An [[if-then-rule|if-then rule]] links a situation you can observe to an action you've already chosen: 'if a trade hits its stop, then I pause 20 minutes.' Psychologists call these implementation intentions (Gollwitzer). The decision is made while you're calm; in the moment, the trigger only has to start it.\"},{\"kind\":\"match\",\"title\":\"Four traps, four if-thens\",\"prompt\":\"The four rules from 'FOMO, revenge and loss aversion', rewritten as if-then rules. Match each trap to its rule.\",\"pairs\":[{\"left\":\"FOMO\",\"right\":\"If a move isn't one of my setups, then I don't trade it.\"},{\"left\":\"Revenge trading\",\"right\":\"If a trade hits its stop, then my next trade risks the usual 1%, after the pause.\",\"wrong\":\"Revenge sizes up after a loss. This if-then fixes the size and puts a pause first.\"},{\"left\":\"Loss aversion\",\"right\":\"If price nears my stop, then I leave the stop where it is.\",\"wrong\":\"Loss aversion moves the stop to avoid the loss. This if-then keeps it where it is.\"},{\"left\":\"Confirmation bias\",\"right\":\"If I'm about to enter, then I first write the price that proves me wrong.\",\"wrong\":\"Confirmation bias filters evidence. Writing the price that proves you wrong, before entry, makes contrary evidence count.\"}],\"concept\":\"if-then-rules\"},{\"kind\":\"order\",\"title\":\"Build a rule\",\"prompt\":\"Put the three steps of a good if-then rule in order.\",\"items\":[\"Name a trigger you can observe: a number, an event or a time\",\"Name the action: pause, skip, close, resize, review or stop for the day\",\"Say how you'll know you did it\"],\"explain\":\"That's the build. 'If I lose two in a row, then I stop for the day, and the journal shows no third trade' passes all three.\",\"concept\":\"if-then-rules\"},{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"Guard-rails\",\"text\":\"Knowing a bias doesn't remove it; structure helps. [[guard-rail|Guard-rails]] are settings that make the right action easy and the wrong one hard: a stop required on every order, a daily lock, a pause timer, preset sizes, a hidden P&L. They make your if-then rules the default, so a tired you doesn't have to remember them.\"},{\"kind\":\"predict\",\"title\":\"Rewrite a vague rule\",\"prompt\":\"Your plan says 'Don't chase.' A normal candle on your chart moves about 0.40. Which rewrite works best as an if-then rule?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Be patient and only take good entries.\",\"feedback\":\"Still vague: 'patient' and 'good' can't be checked. There's no trigger and no action.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"If price is more than one normal candle (0.40) beyond my planned entry, then I don't enter and wait for a pullback.\",\"feedback\":\"Right.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"If I feel FOMO, then I'll try harder to stay calm.\",\"feedback\":\"You can notice the trigger, but 'try harder' isn't an action you can check.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Never buy after a big green candle.\",\"feedback\":\"Closer, but 'big' isn't defined, and there's no action for what to do instead.\"}],\"answer\":\"b\"},\"reveal\":\"The best rewrite gives the trigger a number, 0.40 beyond the entry, and an action you can check: no entry, wait for a pullback. Anyone looking at the chart would agree whether it applied.\",\"concept\":\"if-then-rules\"},{\"kind\":\"fields\",\"title\":\"Your if-then rules\",\"prompt\":\"Three urges, three templates: a losing streak, chasing, and a bad day. Set the numbers that make each trigger observable. They become the if-then section of your plan.\",\"fields\":[{\"id\":\"lossesInRow\",\"label\":\"If I lose __ trades in a row, then I stop for the day\",\"kind\":\"stepper\",\"min\":2,\"max\":5,\"step\":1,\"default\":3},{\"id\":\"chase\",\"label\":\"If price runs more than __ × a normal candle past my entry, then I skip\",\"kind\":\"choice\",\"options\":[0.5,1,2],\"default\":1},{\"id\":\"notFitRisk\",\"label\":\"If I'm not fit to trade, then my risk per trade is\",\"kind\":\"choice\",\"options\":[0,0.5,1],\"unit\":\"%\",\"default\":0.5}],\"valid\":{\"field\":\"notFitRisk\",\"lte\":0.5},\"correct\":\"Saved: three rules, each with a number for the trigger and an action you can check.\",\"feedback\":[{\"when\":{\"field\":\"notFitRisk\",\"gt\":0.5},\"text\":\"1% is your usual risk, so this rule changes nothing. If you're not fit, the action is smaller or nothing: 0.5% or 0%.\"}],\"concept\":\"if-then-rules\"},{\"kind\":\"callout\",\"tone\":\"key\",\"text\":\"Write your if-then rules into your plan. That makes it v1.1.\"}],\"task\":{\"widget\":\"bias-spotter\",\"data\":{\"cards\":[{\"id\":\"r1\",\"text\":\"If a trade hits its stop, then I close the ticket and wait 20 minutes before the next decision.\",\"answer\":\"ready\",\"correct\":\"Ready. An event you can see, and an action you can time.\",\"rationales\":{\"trigger\":\"'Hits its stop' is an event you can see on the ticket. The trigger is clear.\",\"action\":\"'Wait 20 minutes' can be timed and checked. The action is concrete.\"}},{\"id\":\"r2\",\"text\":\"If the market feels risky, then I trade at half size.\",\"answer\":\"trigger\",\"correct\":\"Vague trigger. 'Feels risky' can't be checked. Give it a number, such as a candle twice the normal range.\",\"rationales\":{\"ready\":\"Half size is a clear action, but 'feels risky' isn't something two people would agree on.\",\"action\":\"The action is fine: half size can be checked. The trigger is the vague part.\"}},{\"id\":\"r3\",\"text\":\"If I lose two trades in a row, then I'll be more disciplined.\",\"answer\":\"action\",\"correct\":\"Vague action. 'More disciplined' can't be checked. Try: 'then I stop for the day.'\",\"rationales\":{\"ready\":\"The trigger is clear, but 'more disciplined' isn't an action you can see yourself doing.\",\"trigger\":\"'Two losses in a row' is a count you can check. The action is the vague part.\"}},{\"id\":\"r4\",\"text\":\"If it's 16:00, then I close any open trade and stop for the day.\",\"answer\":\"ready\",\"correct\":\"Ready. A time is the easiest trigger to observe, and both actions can be checked.\",\"rationales\":{\"trigger\":\"A clock time is as observable as a trigger gets.\",\"action\":\"'Close any open trade and stop' are two actions you can check.\"}},{\"id\":\"r5\",\"text\":\"If things go badly today, then I stop trading.\",\"answer\":\"trigger\",\"correct\":\"Vague trigger. 'Badly' has no number. Try: 'If I'm down 2% on the day…'\",\"rationales\":{\"ready\":\"Stopping is a clear action, but 'badly' could mean anything. Give the trigger a number.\",\"action\":\"Stopping is concrete. The trigger, 'badly', is what needs a number.\"}},{\"id\":\"r6\",\"text\":\"If my check-in is 4 or higher, then I try to calm down.\",\"answer\":\"action\",\"correct\":\"Vague action. Try: 'then I pause 15 minutes and run the checklist.'\",\"rationales\":{\"ready\":\"The trigger is a number you can check, but 'try to calm down' isn't an action you can verify.\",\"trigger\":\"'4 or higher' is a number from your check-in, so it's observable. The action is the vague part.\"}}],\"options\":[{\"id\":\"ready\",\"label\":\"Ready to use\"},{\"id\":\"trigger\",\"label\":\"Vague trigger\"},{\"id\":\"action\",\"label\":\"Vague action\"}]},\"goal\":\"Six draft rules for a plan. Check each one: does it have a trigger you can observe and an action you can check?\",\"success\":{\"field\":\"solved\",\"eq\":6},\"correct\":\"Six rules checked. A rule holds when its trigger is a number, an event or a time, and its action comes from a short list: pause, skip, close, resize, review or stop for the day.\",\"hints\":[\"Trigger test: could two people watching the same screen agree it happened?\",\"Action test: could you look back later and check whether you did it?\",\"Words like 'feels', 'badly', 'disciplined' and 'try' mark the vague part.\"],\"showMe\":{\"solved\":6},\"concept\":\"if-then-rules\"},\"check\":[\"q-1.7.6-1\",\"q-1.7.6-2\",\"q-1.7.6-3\"],\"recap\":{\"takeaways\":[\"A good if-then rule names an observable trigger and an action you can check.\",\"Turn each urge into a rule: chasing, losing streaks, bad days.\",\"Rules decided in advance hold better than willpower: the decision is already made.\"],\"next\":\"Next, 1.7.7: the pause, the action behind most of these rules, gets a length you choose and a fixed routine.\"}},\"1.7.7\":{\"unit\":\"1.7\",\"courseId\":\"1.7.7\",\"bridge\":\"In 1.7.2 the rule was: after a loss or a sharp move, pause before any decision. 1.7.6 made rules like that observable. Here the pause gets a length you choose and a fixed routine, so it holds when you most want to skip it.\",\"hook\":{\"prompt\":\"You were stopped out two minutes ago and a setup appears. What does waiting 15 minutes cost you?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"miss\",\"label\":\"I might miss it\"},{\"id\":\"little\",\"label\":\"Very little: a valid setup can wait, or another will come\"},{\"id\":\"winrate\",\"label\":\"My win rate drops\"}],\"answer\":\"little\"},\"compare\":\"You chose “{yours}”. Waiting costs very little: a valid setup is usually still there in 15 minutes, and another will come. What the wait buys is a decision made after the sting of the loss, not during it.\",\"concept\":\"cooling-off-pause\"},\"learn\":[{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"A set pause\",\"text\":\"'FOMO, revenge and loss aversion' gave you the rule: after a loss or a sharp move, pause before any decision. A [[cooling-off-pause|cooling-off pause]] makes it concrete. You set how long it lasts, and you fill it with a fixed routine. A pause with no length and no routine is just a wish to wait.\"},{\"kind\":\"fields\",\"title\":\"Your pause\",\"prompt\":\"Set your pause: long enough to stand up and step away, short enough that you'll keep it. Then set the check-in rating at which you're ready to decide again.\",\"fields\":[{\"id\":\"minutes\",\"label\":\"After a stop-out or a sharp move, I pause __ minutes\",\"kind\":\"stepper\",\"min\":0,\"max\":60,\"step\":5,\"default\":15},{\"id\":\"ready\",\"label\":\"I decide again only when my check-in is __ or lower\",\"kind\":\"stepper\",\"min\":1,\"max\":5,\"step\":1,\"default\":3}],\"valid\":{\"all\":[{\"field\":\"minutes\",\"gte\":5},{\"field\":\"ready\",\"lte\":3}]},\"correct\":\"Saved. This is the pause in your plan, v1.1. Take it every time, not only when the loss hurts.\",\"feedback\":[{\"when\":{\"field\":\"minutes\",\"lt\":5},\"text\":\"Zero minutes is no pause. Pick at least 5: enough to close the ticket, stand up and check in.\"},{\"when\":{\"field\":\"ready\",\"gt\":3},\"text\":\"At 4 or 5 out of 5 the feeling is still strong. The pause is there to let it come down before you decide.\"}],\"concept\":\"cooling-off-pause\"},{\"kind\":\"figure\",\"title\":\"The pause in the decision path\",\"text\":\"Four minutes ago, a stop-out. Now a pullback setup appears: all conditions met, risk 1% of the account. Walk it through.\",\"figure\":{\"kind\":\"flow\",\"start\":\"n0\",\"interactive\":true,\"nodes\":[{\"id\":\"n0\",\"text\":\"Loss or big move just now?\",\"kind\":\"question\",\"yes\":\"pause\",\"no\":\"n1\",\"correct\":\"yes\",\"explain\":\"A stop-out four minutes ago counts. The pause comes first, however good the setup looks.\"},{\"id\":\"pause\",\"text\":\"Pause: your set minutes, then the routine\",\"kind\":\"pause\",\"next\":\"n1\"},{\"id\":\"n1\",\"text\":\"Setup present?\",\"kind\":\"question\",\"yes\":\"n2\",\"no\":\"no-trade-1\",\"correct\":\"yes\"},{\"id\":\"n2\",\"text\":\"All conditions met?\",\"kind\":\"question\",\"yes\":\"n3\",\"no\":\"no-trade-2\",\"correct\":\"yes\",\"explain\":\"The case says all conditions are met. If they still are after the pause, the checklist allows the trade.\"},{\"id\":\"n3\",\"text\":\"Risk within the limit?\",\"kind\":\"question\",\"yes\":\"enter\",\"no\":\"resize\",\"correct\":\"yes\",\"explain\":\"1% of the account as it is now is within the limit.\"},{\"id\":\"no-trade-1\",\"text\":\"No trade\",\"kind\":\"end\"},{\"id\":\"no-trade-2\",\"text\":\"No trade\",\"kind\":\"end\"},{\"id\":\"enter\",\"text\":\"Enter with a stop\",\"kind\":\"end\"},{\"id\":\"resize\",\"text\":\"Resize within the limit, or skip\",\"kind\":\"end\"}],\"steps\":[\"A stop-out four minutes ago: the pause starts. Your timer, then the routine.\",\"After the pause: the setup is still there, every condition still met.\",\"Risk is 1% of the account now: enter with a stop, and write it down.\"]},\"concept\":\"cooling-off-pause\"},{\"kind\":\"order\",\"title\":\"The pause routine\",\"prompt\":\"Fill the pause with the same routine every time. Put it in order.\",\"items\":[\"Close the ticket\",\"Step away from the screen\",\"Check in: rate yourself 1 to 5\",\"Reframe: one loss is one draw from many\",\"Run the checklist\",\"Decide, and write it down\"],\"explain\":\"That's the routine. It ends in a written decision, trade or no trade, so the pause always leads somewhere.\",\"note\":{\"key\":\"1.7.7:routine\",\"label\":\"Your decision after the pause, and its reason (optional)\",\"placeholder\":\"No trade: the setup was gone. Or: entered at 1%, every condition met.\"},\"concept\":\"cooling-off-pause\"},{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"Reframe, don't suppress\",\"text\":\"How you handle the feeling matters. [[reappraisal|Reappraisal]] changes the frame early: 'one loss is one draw from many.' Suppression, 'I feel nothing', fights the feeling after it has grown. In a qualitative study at four London investment banks (Fenton-O'Creevy and colleagues, 2011), higher-performing traders relied more on early strategies like reframing. Small evidence, but a cheap habit.\"},{\"kind\":\"predict\",\"title\":\"Fresh eyes\",\"prompt\":\"You're holding a long at −0.6R. Today's checklist would fail it: the higher lows have broken. If you had no position now, would you open this one, at this size?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"yes\",\"label\":\"Yes\",\"feedback\":\"Run the checklist again: with the higher lows broken, condition 1 fails. You wouldn't open this trade today.\"},{\"id\":\"no\",\"label\":\"No\",\"feedback\":\"Right.\"}],\"answer\":\"no\"},\"reveal\":\"When the answer is no, exit or reduce, and write the decision down. What you've already lost doesn't change what the trade is worth now: that's the sunk-cost trap from 'FOMO, revenge and loss aversion'.\",\"concept\":\"loss-aversion\"},{\"kind\":\"callout\",\"tone\":\"info\",\"text\":\"The pause is a rule, not a punishment. Your daily loss limit is its long version.\"}],\"task\":{\"widget\":\"position-size\",\"goal\":\"The pause is over and the setup passed the checklist. After the stop-out the account is 9,900.00. The ticket still shows the urge: 94 units. Size it at your plan's 1%, costs included.\",\"start\":{\"account\":9900,\"riskPct\":1,\"entry\":100,\"stop\":98,\"units\":94},\"locked\":[\"account\",\"riskPct\",\"entry\",\"stop\"],\"hidden\":[\"simpleQty\",\"qty\"],\"success\":{\"field\":\"units\",\"eq\":47},\"feedback\":[{\"when\":{\"field\":\"units\",\"gt\":49},\"text\":\"{units} units lose {lossAtStop:2} at the stop, far over the 99.00 budget. That's the size of wanting it back, not the plan's.\"},{\"when\":{\"field\":\"units\",\"eq\":49},\"text\":\"49 is the textbook size: 99.00 ÷ 2.00. With both commissions it loses 102.86, over 99.00.\"},{\"when\":{\"field\":\"units\",\"eq\":48},\"text\":\"Close. 48 units lose 100.76 at the stop, 1.76 over the budget. Round down.\"},{\"when\":{\"field\":\"units\",\"lt\":47},\"text\":\"That fits the budget, but a larger size still would. Find the largest that stays within 99.00.\"}],\"correct\":\"Right. 47 units lose 98.66 at the stop, costs included: within 1% of 9,900.00. The same risk as always, on the account as it is now.\",\"hints\":[\"Start from the budget: 1% of 9,900.00 is 99.00, and the stop is 2.00 away.\",\"Each unit also pays commission at entry and at the stop: about 0.10 per unit.\",\"99.00 ÷ 2.099 ≈ 47.2. Round down.\"],\"showMe\":{\"units\":47},\"concept\":\"risk-per-trade\"},\"check\":[\"q-1.7.7-1\",\"q-1.7.7-2\",\"q-1.7.7-3\"],\"recap\":{\"takeaways\":[\"Set your pause length in the plan, and take it after every loss or sharp move.\",\"Fill the pause with the routine: step away, check in, reframe, run the checklist, decide.\",\"Ask with fresh eyes: would I open this trade now? If not, exit or reduce.\"],\"next\":\"Next: the 1.7 checkpoint. Then unit 1.8, Danger zone: martingale, grids, scams and leverage, the ways accounts blow up.\"}}}");
